---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import VersionBumper from '../../components/VersionBumper.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Semantic Version Bump Calculator" description="See the next major, minor, and patch version numbers from your current version.">
  <VersionBumper client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why bump type is a communication decision, not just a number</h2>
  <p>Semantic versioning's real value is communicating expectations to consumers of your package — a patch bump signals "safe to upgrade automatically," while a major bump signals "review before upgrading," making the correct bump type as important as the code change itself.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your current version number.</li>
    <li>See the next patch, minor, and major version options.</li>
    <li>Choose based on the nature of your change (bug fix, new feature, breaking change).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>From version 1.4.2: a patch bump gives 1.4.3, a minor bump gives 1.5.0, and a major bump gives 2.0.0.</p>

  <h2>Common Version Bump Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bumping minor for a breaking change</strong>, misleading consumers into thinking it's safe to upgrade automatically when it isn't.</li>
    <li><strong>Bumping major too liberally</strong> for minor breaking changes, causing unnecessary version number inflation and consumer hesitation to upgrade.</li>
    <li><strong>Not resetting lower version components</strong> — a minor bump resets patch to 0, and a major bump resets both minor and patch to 0.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Version Bumper</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Deciding the correct version number for a new package release</li>
    <li>Understanding what version bump type a specific change warrants</li>
    <li>Communicating change impact clearly to package consumers via version number</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Semantic Version Bump FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What counts as a "breaking change"?</strong> Any change that could cause existing correct usage of your package to fail or behave differently — removing a function, changing a function's signature, or altering expected output format are common examples.</p>
</ToolLayout>
